# Code Comment Standards

## Philosophy
Comments explain **why** and clarify the non-obvious. They do not narrate what
the code clearly does. When in doubt, omit.

---

## 0. Preservation
- Never delete a comment that already exists in the code.
- Minor modifications are allowed: fixing grammar, improving clarity, or
  reformatting to fit these rules — as long as the original purpose and meaning
  are fully preserved.
- When in doubt, leave it exactly as-is.

---

## 1. File Header Comment
One line, at the very top of every file.

Format:  `// <filename>.<ext> — <purpose of this file/module/class>`

Examples:
  // main_traclus_dl.rs — orchestrates the full TraclusDL pipeline (load, cluster, output)
  // auth.service.ts — handles JWT issuance, validation, and refresh logic

Rules:
- Describe the file's purpose, not its contents list
- One line only, no period at the end

---

## 2. Function / Method Comment
Placed directly above the function signature, no blank line between comment and function.

Format:
  // <what this function does> (single line if simple)
  // <additional context if the function is complex or long> (up to 2 more lines)

Rules:
- Only describe WHAT it does and WHY if not obvious
- Never describe parameters, return types, or anything the signature already says
- Omit entirely for small, self-evident functions (getters, setters, simple
  constructors, one-liners)
- More than 1 line only if the function is long or has non-obvious behavior

---

## 3. Section Comment (inside a function)
Use MINIMALLY. Only when:
  a) The code block's intent is not immediately clear, OR
  b) Breaking a long function into named logical sections

Format:  `// <section label or brief explanation>`

Rules:
- Placed on the line directly above the section, never inline
- No trailing punctuation
- One line only

---

## 4. Inline (Same-Line) Comment
Use MINIMALLY. 
For properties, constants, or fields whose name alone is insufficient.

Format:  `<code>  // <short clarification>`  (2 spaces before `//`)
Max ~60 characters for the comment text.

Examples:
  const BUCKET_SIZE: f64 = 0.5;  // degrees, must evenly divide 360.0
  pub angle_start: f64,          // (inclusive)
  pub angle_end: f64,            // (exclusive)

Rules:
- Never use for obvious values or self-describing names
- Align across related fields for readability

---

## 5. Special Tags
Always in this exact format. Never removed.

  // TODO: <what needs to be done and why>
  // FIXME: <what is broken and known impact>
  // HACK: <what shortcut was taken and what the proper fix would be>

---

## 6. Commented-Out Code
Flag with a `// DEAD:` tag directly above, with a reason. Do not remove.

  // DEAD: <reason — e.g. replaced by X, pending removal, kept for reference>
  // <original commented-out code>

---

## 7. No Redundant Narration
Never write comments that translate the code:
  ✗  // loop through items
  ✗  // set the value
  ✗  // return the result

---

## 8. Language Adaptation
Apply all rules above using the comment syntax of the file's language.
Multi-line comments may use the language's block syntax when natural.
